winemac: in background mode, keep the program's threads at the priority of a visible app.

HADRON_MAC_BACKGROUND=1 (0056) puts every window under the desktop. macOS then takes the program
for an app nobody can see and, about 20 seconds after it starts, clamps all its threads to utility
QoS (priority 20, "20T" in ps -M), although winemac holds a user-initiated activity against App
Nap. A Mac test program with a window at the lowest level, never activated, shows the same: no
activity 4T (App Nap), user-initiated allowing idle sleep 20T, the same plus latency-critical
46T/31T as at the start, 40 seconds on. Latency-critical makes no power assertion; idle system
sleep stays allowed. Background mode now always takes the activity, App Nap enabled or not.

Without background mode: no change.

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m
@@ -260,9 +260,17 @@
             if (activateIfTransformed && !macdrv_background_mode())
                 [self tryToActivateIgnoringOtherApps:YES];
 
-            if (!enable_app_nap)
+            if (!enable_app_nap || macdrv_background_mode())
             {
-                [[[NSProcessInfo processInfo] beginActivityWithOptions:NSActivityUserInitiatedAllowingIdleSystemSleep
+                NSActivityOptions options = NSActivityUserInitiatedAllowingIdleSystemSleep;
+
+                /* Background mode puts every window under the desktop, so macOS takes the app
+                   for one nobody can see and, a few seconds on, clamps all its threads to utility
+                   QoS (priority 20) despite the user-initiated activity. Latency-critical keeps
+                   them where a visible app's are; idle system sleep stays allowed. */
+                if (macdrv_background_mode())
+                    options |= NSActivityLatencyCritical;
+                [[[NSProcessInfo processInfo] beginActivityWithOptions:options
                                                                 reason:@"Running Windows program"] retain]; // intentional leak
             }
 
